File: force-app/main/default/lwc/collapsibleSection/collapsibleSection.html
```html
<template>
    <button
        class="section-toggle"
        aria-expanded={isExpanded ? 'true' : 'false'}
        aria-controls={bodyId}
        onclick={toggle}
    >
        <slot name="title"></slot>
    </button>
    <div class="section-body" id={bodyId} hidden={!isExpanded}>
        <slot></slot>
    </div>
</template>
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Unique `id` per instance** | A module-level counter incremented in the constructor guarantees every instance gets a distinct `id`, so `aria-controls` always resolves to the correct element. |
| **`hidden` attribute toggling** | LWC treats boolean attributes (`hidden`, `disabled`, …) specially: binding `true` renders the attribute, binding `false` removes it. `hidden={!isExpanded}` therefore gives exactly the required behaviour—attribute present when collapsed, absent when expanded—while the `<div>` stays in the DOM at all times. |
| **`aria-expanded` as a string** | `aria-*` attributes are string-typed in ARIA; the explicit ternary `'true' : 'false'` avoids any ambiguity about how LWC serialises a raw boolean into an ARIA attribute. |
| **Slots** | `<slot name="title">` inside the button captures the named slot; `<slot>` (unnamed) inside the body div captures default content. |

