```apex
public class AccountOwnershipService {

    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, OwnerId
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ];

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, OwnerId
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
        ];

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            acc.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
        }

        for (Contact con : contacts) {
            con.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
        }

        TriggerActionBypass.bypass(Account.SObjectType, () => {
            update accounts;
            update contacts;
        });
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Requirement | How it's satisfied |
|---|---|
| No Account trigger actions | Both DMLs are wrapped in `TriggerActionBypass.bypass(Account.SObjectType, …)`. This ensures that even if a Contact trigger action cascades back to an Account, Account actions remain suppressed. |
| Contact trigger actions run normally | The bypass targets only `Account.SObjectType`; Contact actions are unaffected. |
| Bypass released on success *or* failure | TAF's `bypass()` method internally uses try/finally to restore the previous bypass state, so the flag is always cleared before the method returns or the exception propagates. |
| All-or-none, exceptions propagate | No `try/catch` in the service; a failure in either `update` rolls back both and the exception is re-thrown to the caller unchanged. |
| ≤ 2 SOQL, ≤ 2 DML | One SOQL for Accounts, one for Contacts; one bulk `update` for each list. No SOQL or DML inside a loop. |
| Bulk-safe (up to 200) | All collections are operated on in a single statement regardless of size. |

